*Figure 1. The 40 points (black), OLS line (blue) with the 95% confidence band of the fitted line (shaded), and the true line (dashed grey). Generated by `figures/make_fit.py`.*

## Interpretation
- The slope is estimated to be 0.72 per unit x, with a 95% interval of about 0.64 to 0.81.
- The intercept is estimated at 4.37 with an interval of 2.44 to 6.31. It is the less precise parameter (SE 0.96 against 0.042 for the slope) because x = 0 lies at the edge of the data, far from the mean of x.
- Both intervals contain the true values, but not by a wide margin: the true slope 0.8 is just inside the upper bound (0.806), and the true intercept 2.5 is just inside the lower bound (2.436). The estimated slope is below truth and the estimated intercept above it. This is what a single 95% interval allows; one draw says nothing about the coverage rate of the method.
- The residual SD 3.08 is consistent with the generating sigma = 3.

## Reproduction and checks
`python3 code/fit.py` (numpy, scipy) regenerates `results/`; `python3 figures/make_fit.py` regenerates the figure (additionally needs matplotlib; the versions used for the figure, matplotlib 3.11.2 among them, are in [`figures/versions.txt`](../figures/versions.txt)). Limitations: noise-draw convention (one `normal` call of size 40, after x is built) is an interpretation of the task; a different draw order would change the numbers. Residual diagnostics beyond the SD were not part of the request.
